    ],
    "bottomLine": "A solid L14-30 inlet with a proper hinged door, and the logical inlet if you already own an EmerGen switch.",
    "description": "Connecticut Electric's 30-amp inlet is the one sold alongside its EmerGen transfer switch kits, and it makes sense on its own if you want a door that closes over the inlet. Its listing describes a 30-amp, NEMA 3R power inlet box with a hinged door, for generators up to 7,500 running watts, and ETL listed. Connecticut Electric's installation sheet for the EmerGen inlet calls it a raintight L14-30 inlet and says it is for outdoor installation only, so unlike the Reliance PB30 it should not go on an inside garage wall. Home Depot and Orgill sell it under the model number EGSPI30, and the older manufacturer cut sheet calls it the PI30, so do not be thrown if the box in your hand carries the shorter number. We found less published detail on knockouts and internal space than for the Reliance boxes, so the electrician should check the instructions in the carton. If you already have an EmerGen G2 switch, this is the matching part."
  },
  {
    "id": "reliance-pbn50",
    "name": "Reliance Controls PBN50 50-Amp Non-Metallic Power Inlet Box",
    "category": "50A, CS6375, ABS, NEMA 3R",
    "badge": "Best 50 Amp Inlet",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "50-amp CS6375 inlet rated for generators up to 12,500 running watts",
      "UV-stabilized ABS NEMA 3R housing with a flip-lid cover",
      "Bottom-entry cord inlet keeps rain off the connector",
      "cULus listed, five-year warranty per Home Depot Canada"
