  },
  {
    "id": "circlecord-gec43025ft",
    "name": "CircleCord GEC43025FT 25 ft 30-Amp Cord and Inlet Box Kit",
    "category": "30A Kit: L14-30 Inlet Box + 25 ft 10-Gauge Cord",
    "badge": "Best Kit With Cord",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Inlet box and matching 25-foot cord in one purchase",
      "Cord listed as 10-gauge SJTW with L14-30P and L14-30R ends",
      "Listed as ETL listed and rated 125/250 volts, 7,500 watts",
      "25 feet lets the generator sit well away from the house"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Third-party brand without the track record of Reliance or Generac",
      "You still need a transfer switch or interlock and an electrician"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The kit to buy if you need both an inlet and a cord, mainly because 25 feet of cord keeps the exhaust away from the house.",
    "description": "Most kits on Amazon pair an inlet box with a 10-foot cord, which is the wrong length for most houses. This CircleCord kit comes with 25 feet, and that is the reason it makes this list. Its listing describes a 30-amp, 125/250 volt kit rated for 7,500 watts, with a 10-gauge SJTW cord with L14-30P and L14-30R twist-lock ends, and an L14-30 inlet box, ETL listed. Twenty-five feet lets the generator sit far enough from the wall that exhaust is not drawn back toward doors and windows, which a 10-foot cord does not. The cord is the part of a generator hookup that gets dragged through mud, run over and left in the sun, so buying a quality cord matters more than shaving a few dollars off the box. CircleCord does not have Reliance's track record, so if you prefer a known name, buy a Reliance PB30 and a separate Reliance 25-foot cord. Either way the kit is only the outside half of the job: the inlet still has to be wired to a transfer switch or an interlocked breaker."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Generator Inlet Box",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-generator-inlet-box/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size generator inlet box do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Match the generator's largest 120/240 volt outlet. If it has an L14-30R twist-lock, which most 5,000 to 9,000 watt portables do, you need a 30-amp L14-30 inlet rated for about 7,500 watts. If it has a 50-amp outlet and you want to use it, you need a 50-amp inlet rated for about 12,500 watts. House size does not change the answer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I install a generator inlet box myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In most places it needs a permit, and many jurisdictions require a licensed electrician because the job connects a power source to your service panel. Even where homeowners can pull their own permit, the inlet must be wired to a transfer switch or listed interlock and inspected. Mounting the box is the easy part; the panel work is where mistakes become dangerous."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a transfer switch with a generator inlet box?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You need either a transfer switch or a listed breaker interlock. The inlet is just a socket. Without transfer equipment behind it, generator power can flow back onto the utility lines. An interlock is usually cheaper and lets you run any circuit, while a manual transfer switch limits you to the circuits wired into it but makes overloading harder."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a plastic inlet box as good as a metal one?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homes, yes. Non-metallic boxes like the Generac 6337 and Reliance PBN30 are rated NEMA 3R like the steel PB30 and will not rust, which makes them better near salt air or salted roads. Steel resists impact better. Either is fine if it is listed, rated NEMA 3R and mounted where the cord cannot be tripped over."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How far from the house should the generator be when plugged into the inlet?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Manufacturers and safety agencies commonly cite at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents. That is why cord length matters: a 10-foot cord forces the generator too close in most layouts. Plan the generator spot first, then choose the inlet location and a 20 to 25-foot cord to match."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I plug a 50 amp generator into a 30 amp inlet?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not safely with an improvised adapter. The cord would be protected only by the generator's 50-amp breaker while carrying a 30-amp-rated connection. If the generator has an L14-30R as well as a 50-amp outlet, use the L14-30R with a 30-amp cord. If you want the full output, install a 50-amp inlet and matching transfer equipment."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between a power inlet box and a transfer switch?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The inlet box is the outdoor socket the generator cord plugs into. The transfer switch, or interlock, is the indoor equipment that disconnects the house from the grid and connects it to the generator instead. You need both. Some kits, like the Generac HomeLink 6853, include an inlet box with the switch, so check before buying one separately."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
