    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Buy the inlet that matches your generator's biggest 240 volt outlet, mount it where the generator can sit safely, and have an electrician wire it to a transfer switch or interlock. For most homes that means a 30-amp L14-30 inlet like the Reliance PB30. A 50-amp inlet is only worth it on a generator with a real 50-amp outlet.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Generator Inlet Box Does, and What It Does Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            A generator inlet box is the weatherproof socket on the outside wall of your house where the generator cord plugs in. It has a male inlet, the mirror image of a plug, mounted in a small enclosure, and its back is wired through the wall to transfer equipment beside your electrical panel. It turns the generator into something you can connect with one cord in the rain instead of a nest of extension cords through a cracked window.
          </p>

          <p>
            What it does not do is make the connection safe by itself. An inlet box is just a socket; without a transfer switch or a breaker interlock behind it, power from the generator can flow back out through your service and onto the utility lines, where it can injure line workers and destroy the generator when the grid returns. Every legal setup has three parts: the inlet outside, transfer equipment inside, and a cord between the generator and the inlet. This page is about the first part. For the inside half, see our picks for the <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">best manual transfer switch</a>, and for how the parts are chosen and what an electrician charges, our guide to <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch types, cost and installation</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The inlet is also the cheapest part of the system, typically well under a hundred dollars for a name-brand 30-amp box, which is why people underthink it. But it decides more than its price suggests. The inlet's plug shape sets which generators can connect, its amperage caps how much of the generator you can use, and where it is mounted decides where the generator has to sit during an outage. Get those three right and the rest of the job is routine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installing an inlet box is electrical work that needs a permit in most places, and in many jurisdictions it must be done by a licensed electrician. It involves a hole through the wall, a wire run to the panel, a two-pole breaker and the transfer equipment. We cover what to tell the electrician later on, but we do not walk through the wiring, because this is not a job to learn on.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Match the inlet to the generator's biggest 240V outlet, not to the house.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">An L14-30R outlet on the generator means a 30-amp L14-30 inlet. A 50-amp outlet means a 50-amp inlet. The panel, the house size and the circuits you want do not change that.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

