        <div id="thirty-vs-fifty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. 30 Amp L14-30 vs 50 Amp: Picking the Right Inlet
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start at the generator, not the house. Look for its largest 120/240 volt outlet. On most portable generators between roughly 5,000 and 9,000 running watts that is an L14-30R, the round four-prong twist-lock rated 30 amps. On larger generators, usually 10,000 running watts and up, there is also a 50-amp outlet. Your inlet should match whichever of those you plan to use. A generator with both outlets can use either; a generator with only an L14-30R can only ever deliver 30 amps through a cord, no matter what inlet you install.
          </p>

          <p>
            The math sets the ceiling. A 30-amp circuit at 240 volts is 7,200 watts, which is why Reliance and Generac rate their 30-amp inlets for generators up to about 7,500 running watts. A 50-amp circuit at 240 volts is 12,000 watts, and the 50-amp inlets here are rated for about 12,500. If your generator runs 6,500 watts, a 50-amp inlet adds cost without adding a single usable watt. If it runs 11,000 watts, a 30-amp inlet throws away a third of it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Generator outlet</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Inlet to buy</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cord</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Usable power at 240V</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">L14-30R (30A twist-lock)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">L14-30 inlet: Reliance PB30 or PBN30, Generac 6337, Connecticut Electric EGSPI30</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">L14-30P to L14-30R, 10-gauge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7,200 W</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">50A four-prong twist-lock</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50A CS6375 inlet: Reliance PBN50 or Generac 6338</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50A generator cord matched to both ends, 6-gauge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 12,000 W</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">14-50R (straight-blade 50A)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50A inlet as above</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">14-50P to 50A twist-lock connector cord</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 12,000 W</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">L5-30R or TT-30R only (120V)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not one of these 240V inlets</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Talk to the electrician first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Half the panel at best</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The 50-amp side is where the plug names get confusing. Many 50-amp generator outlets and inlets use the twist-lock design often called California Style, sold under part numbers such as CS6369 for the generator receptacle, CS6365 for the cord plug, CS6364 for the cord connector and CS6375 for the inlet. Other generators, especially newer ones marketed for RVs, use a straight-blade 14-50R instead. The inlet does not change between those two cases; the cord does. A generator with a 14-50R needs a cord with a 14-50P on one end and the 50-amp twist-lock connector on the other. If you are still choosing a generator, our guide to the <a href="/best-generator-with-50-amp-outlet/">best generators with a 50 amp outlet</a> covers which outlet each model uses.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not let a salesperson or a forum talk you into an adapter that steps a 50-amp generator down into a 30-amp inlet, or into putting a 50-amp inlet on a 30-amp generator "for later." The first leaves the cord protected only by the generator's 50-amp breaker. The second spends money on wire and parts the generator can never use. Buy the inlet for the generator you have, or for the one you have actually chosen.
          </p>

          <p>
            The one case where none of these inlets fits is a generator with only 120 volt outlets, such as an L5-30R or a TT-30R RV outlet and nothing larger. Those generators cannot feed both legs of a split-phase panel, so 240 volt loads like a well pump will not run at all. If that is your generator, ask the electrician before buying any inlet, and consider whether a <a href="/best-240v-generator/">240V generator</a> is the better spend.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Steel, Aluminum or Plastic: Enclosures and NEMA 3R
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every inlet on this list is rated NEMA 3R. That rating means the enclosure protects against falling rain, sleet and snow and against ice forming on the outside, which is the right level for a box on the side of a house. It is not a submersion or hose-down rating, so the inlet still needs to sit above the splash line and away from a downspout outlet. If a retailer's listing does not say NEMA 3R or raintight, it is not an inlet box for an outside wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inside that rating, the choice is metal or non-metallic. Powder-coated steel, as on the Reliance PB30, is stiff and takes knocks well, but the coating eventually chips, and once it does a steel box rusts. Non-metallic housings, the glass-filled nylon of the Generac 6337 and the UV-stabilized ABS of the Reliance PBN30 and PBN50, will not rust at all and are the better choice within a few miles of salt water or beside a road that gets salted every winter. Aluminum boxes such as Generac's 6343 sit in between, corrosion resistant and stiff, but they cost more and offer no real advantage for most homes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plastic's weak point is impact. A heavy 10-gauge or 6-gauge cord locked into the inlet acts as a lever, and if someone trips over it or a mower catches it, the force goes into the box and the screws holding it to the wall. Mount a non-metallic inlet where the cord can drop straight down rather than across a path, and route the cord so it does not hang at an angle in a high-traffic spot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cord entry direction is the other practical difference. Front-entry boxes such as the PB30 and Generac 6337 have the inlet on the face, so the cord sticks straight out from the wall. Bottom-entry boxes such as the PBN30 and PBN50 point the inlet down, so rain runs off the connector and the cord hangs vertically. Bottom entry is better for weather but needs clear space below the box. Neither is wrong; pick the one that suits the wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            A hinged or flip-lid cover is standard, and on the inlets here it closes over the inlet when nothing is plugged in. It matters more than it seems. An inlet that sits uncovered for years collects spider webs, wasp nests and grit in the contacts, and the first time you need it you find out. Close the lid after every test, and lift it once a season to check the contacts are clean.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pairing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How the Inlet Pairs With an Interlock or a Transfer Switch
          </h2>

          <p>
            Behind the wall, the inlet connects to one of two kinds of transfer equipment, and the inlet itself is the same in both cases. With a manual transfer switch, the inlet is wired to a small subpanel beside the main panel, and a handful of chosen circuits are moved into that subpanel. With a breaker interlock, the inlet is wired to a two-pole breaker in the main panel itself, and a sliding plate makes it physically impossible for that breaker and the main breaker to be on at the same time. Either way, the inlet's amperage should match the breaker or switch it feeds.
          </p>

          <p>
            A transfer switch limits you to the circuits wired into it, usually six to ten, but makes it hard to overload the generator because you picked those circuits for it. Some switches, like the Reliance Pro/Tran 2 models, add watt meters so you can see the load on each leg. An interlock lets you power any circuit in the panel, which is more flexible and usually cheaper in parts, but leaves it to you to keep the load under the generator's rating. Our <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">manual transfer switch picks</a> compare both, including the Siemens interlock and the Reliance and Generac switches that pair with these inlets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some switch kits already include an inlet box. The Generac HomeLink 6853 kit ships with an L14-30 resin inlet and a cord, and Connecticut Electric's EmerGen G2 kits include a NEMA 3R inlet and a cord in 10, 25 or 50-foot lengths. If you are buying one of those kits, you do not need a separate inlet from this list unless you want to upgrade the box itself. Reliance's 50-amp 510C switch does not include an inlet, which is exactly where the PBN50 fits. Reliance's outdoor R510A has the 50-amp inlet built into the switch enclosure, so no separate box is needed at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brand matching is less important than people think. An L14-30 inlet is an L14-30 inlet, and a Reliance box works with a Generac switch or a Siemens interlock as long as the electrician sizes the wire and breaker correctly. The reason to match brands is convenience, a single instruction sheet and a single warranty contact, not compatibility.
          </p>

          <p>
            The one wiring question that does depend on the generator is the neutral. Most portable generators bond their neutral to the frame. If your transfer equipment does not switch the neutral, which most residential manual switches and every interlock do not, a bonded generator creates a second neutral-to-ground bond, and that can trip the generator's GFCI outlets or create objectionable current on the ground wire. The fix depends on the generator and the local inspector, and it is covered in the neutral bonding section of our guide on <a href="/how-to-hook-up-a-generator-to-your-house/">how to hook up a generator to your house</a>. Ask the electrician about it before the install, not after.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Where to Mount the Inlet: Cord Length and Exhaust
          </h2>

          <p>
            The inlet decides where the generator sits, and that makes placement the most important safety decision in the whole job. A generator must run outdoors, away from doors, windows and vents, with the exhaust pointed away from the house. Manufacturers and safety agencies commonly cite at least 20 feet. If the inlet is beside the back door and the cord is 10 feet long, the generator ends up 10 feet from the back door, every outage, for the life of the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            So plan the generator's spot first, then put the inlet within reach of it. Pick a level, well-drained spot on a wall with no windows or vents that open to bedrooms or living areas, ideally on the downwind side of the house for your prevailing winds. Then mount the inlet on the nearest wall and buy a cord long enough to put the generator in that spot. That is why we rank a kit with a 25-foot cord above the more common 10-foot kits. Our guide on <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> goes through clearances and covers in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cord length has a cost, but at these lengths it is small. A 25-foot 10-gauge cord at 30 amps loses a fraction of a volt more than a 10-foot cord, which no appliance will notice. Past 50 feet, or with a thinner cord, voltage drop starts to matter for motors like a well pump. Our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">generator extension cord size guide</a> covers gauge by length if you need a longer run.
          </p>

          <p>
            Height matters too. Mount the inlet high enough to stay above snow and splash, but low enough that the cord does not hang taut from the box. Keep it away from the outlet of a downspout and from a spot where a sprinkler hits it. On the inside, the shorter the wire run to the panel, the cheaper the job, which is why many inlets end up directly behind the panel on an outside wall. That is fine as long as the generator spot on that side of the house still meets the clearance rules. If it does not, pay for the longer wire run.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Set the Job</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">7,200 W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">30A at 240V ceiling</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">12,000 W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">50A at 240V ceiling</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 ft+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Commonly cited generator distance from the house</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">NEMA 3R</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum rating for an outdoor inlet</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="kits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Inlet Kits With a Cord: When They Make Sense
          </h2>

          <p>
            Search Amazon for a generator inlet box and most of what comes back are kits: an inlet box and a cord sold together, usually from brands you have not heard of. They can be good value, because a quality 30-amp cord is often the more expensive half of the pair. But they are only worth it if both halves are right for your house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the cord length first. The 10-foot cord in most kits is useful only if the inlet itself is on a wall where the generator can safely sit 10 feet away, which in most homes it cannot. A 20 or 25-foot cord suits most houses. Then check the gauge: a 30-amp cord should be 10-gauge with four conductors, often written 10/4, and a 50-amp cord should be 6-gauge or heavier. Thinner cords sold as 30-amp are a fire risk at full load.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, check for a listing mark from a nationally recognized testing lab, such as UL, ETL or CSA, on both the cord and the inlet. The name-brand inlets here are UL or cULus listed, and the CircleCord kit is listed as ETL listed. Inspectors look for that mark, and an unlisted inlet can fail an inspection even when it is wired perfectly. A kit with no listing mark in the listing photos should be skipped regardless of price.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own a good cord with the right ends, skip the kit and buy only the inlet. If you are buying a transfer switch kit like the Generac HomeLink 6853 or Connecticut Electric's EmerGen G2 that already includes an inlet and cord, skip this purchase altogether. Kits are the right answer only when you are wiring an interlock or a bare switch and own nothing else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Generator Inlet Box Install: Permits and What to Ask the Electrician
          </h2>

          <p>
            Generator inlet installation is permit work in most of the US. The job connects a power source to your service, and the inspector's job is to confirm that power cannot backfeed the grid, that the wire and breaker match the inlet, and that the enclosure is right for its location. In many jurisdictions the work must be done by a licensed electrician; in some, a homeowner may pull a permit for their own house. Either way, the inspection is not optional, and some insurers and utilities ask whether the connection was permitted after a claim or an incident.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of the cost is labor, not the inlet. The electrician has to drill through the wall, run cable or conduit to the panel, install a two-pole breaker and either an interlock plate or a transfer switch, and in a switch install move each chosen circuit into the switch. A short run straight through the wall behind the panel is the cheapest case. A long run through a finished basement is the expensive one. Our guide to <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch cost and installation</a> breaks down what drives the quote.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to tell the electrician</h3>

          <p>
            Get two or three quotes, and give each electrician the same information so the quotes are comparable: the generator model and its exact 240 volt outlet, the inlet you have bought or want, interlock or switch, the circuits you want powered, and where you want the generator to sit. Ask whether the quote includes the permit, the inlet box, the breaker and the interlock kit, because quotes that look far apart often differ in what they include rather than in the hourly rate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask three specific questions. First, what wire gauge and type they plan to use, and whether the inlet's terminals are rated for it; this matters most on the 50-amp boxes. Second, how they will handle the generator's neutral bond, given your generator and transfer equipment. Third, whether your local code adds anything beyond the basic inlet, breaker and interlock, such as an outdoor disconnect or surge protection, which some newer code editions add for certain installations. A good electrician will have answers ready.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once it passes inspection, test it. Run the generator through the inlet for half an hour with real loads before you need it, and repeat that before every storm season. An inlet that has never had a cord in it is one you do not know works.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-generator" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Generators Need Which Inlet
          </h2>

          <p>
            The generator you own, or the one you are about to buy, decides the inlet. Most generators in the 5,000 to 8,000 running-watt range, including everything on our list of the <a href="/best-6500-watt-generator/">best 6500 watt generators</a> and most of our <a href="/best-5000-watt-generator/">5,000 watt</a> and <a href="/best-8000-watt-generator/">8,000 watt</a> picks, have an L14-30R. For them, a 30-amp L14-30 inlet is correct and a 50-amp inlet is wasted money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Generators from about 10,000 running watts up usually add a 50-amp outlet, and that is where the PBN50 or Generac 6338 earn their place. A generator rated 11,000 or 12,000 watts behind a 30-amp inlet is limited to about 7,200 watts, enough for essentials but not enough to run a central air conditioner alongside them. If your plan includes central air, an electric water heater or a well pump plus everything else, you need both the larger generator and the 50-amp inlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inverter generators follow the same rule. A larger inverter with an L14-30R takes the same L14-30 inlet as an open-frame generator. Smaller inverters, around 2,000 to 4,500 watts, usually have only 120 volt outlets and are not suited to an inlet at all; those generators are better used with extension cords to individual appliances. If you are still choosing a generator around the inlet, start with our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generators</a> guide, then come back here for the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole-house standby generators do not use an inlet box at all. They are wired permanently through an automatic transfer switch, and the work is priced and permitted differently. If you are weighing a portable generator and inlet against a standby unit, our <a href="/whole-house-generator-installation-cost/">whole-house generator installation cost</a> guide sets out what the standby route costs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When an Inlet Box Is Not Worth It
          </h2>

          <p>
            An inlet box is worth it for most people who own a 240 volt portable generator and lose power more than once every few years. But there are cases where it is the wrong spend. If you own a small 120 volt inverter and only want to keep a refrigerator and a few lamps running, two heavy-duty extension cords run directly to those appliances do the job without a permit or an electrician.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you rent, the panel is not yours to modify, and an inlet is a landlord decision. If your panel is old, full, or a brand with no listed interlock kit, the inlet might be the cheap part of a much larger job that includes a panel replacement; get the electrician's view of the panel before committing. And if your outages are long and frequent enough that you are refuelling a portable generator for days every winter, a standby generator might be the better long-term answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, the inlet box is the part that turns a generator in the garage into actual backup power. It is cheap, and its job is narrow: be the right plug, in the right box, in the right place on the wall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 50-amp inlet for a 30-amp generator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the generator's biggest 240 volt outlet is an L14-30R, it can never push more than 30 amps through the cord. A 50-amp inlet, 6-gauge wire and a 50-amp cord cost noticeably more and add nothing. Match the inlet to the outlet you will actually use, and size up only when you buy a generator with a 50-amp outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mounting the inlet where the generator cannot safely sit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The inlet location decides the generator location. Putting it beside a back door or under a bedroom window because that is closest to the panel means running a generator there in every outage. Choose the generator's spot first, at a safe distance from openings, then put the inlet within cord reach of that spot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing an inlet with no transfer equipment</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An inlet wired straight to a breaker with no interlock or switch can backfeed the utility lines, endangering line workers and wrecking the generator when power returns. The inlet is only legal and safe as part of a system with a transfer switch or a listed interlock, installed under a permit and inspected.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a thin or unlisted cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 30-amp generator cord should be 10-gauge, four-conductor, with listed L14-30 ends; a 50-amp cord should be 6-gauge or heavier. Cheap cords sold as 30-amp with thinner wire heat up at full load. The cord takes the most abuse in the system, so it is the worst place to save money.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the inlet untested for years</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An inlet that has never had a cord in it collects grit, insects and corrosion in its contacts, and a lid left open makes it worse. Plug in and run the generator through the inlet for half an hour with real loads every year, ideally before storm season, and keep the cover closed in between.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator inlet box do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match the generator's largest 120/240 volt outlet. If it has an L14-30R twist-lock, which most 5,000 to 9,000 watt portables do, you need a 30-amp L14-30 inlet rated for about 7,500 watts. If it has a 50-amp outlet and you want to use it, you need a 50-amp inlet rated for about 12,500 watts. House size does not change the answer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a generator inlet box myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most places it needs a permit, and many jurisdictions require a licensed electrician because the job connects a power source to your service panel. Even where homeowners can pull their own permit, the inlet must be wired to a transfer switch or listed interlock and inspected. Mounting the box is the easy part; the panel work is where mistakes become dangerous.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a transfer switch with a generator inlet box?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You need either a transfer switch or a listed breaker interlock. The inlet is just a socket. Without transfer equipment behind it, generator power can flow back onto the utility lines. An interlock is usually cheaper and lets you run any circuit, while a manual transfer switch limits you to the circuits wired into it but makes overloading harder.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a plastic inlet box as good as a metal one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, yes. Non-metallic boxes like the Generac 6337 and Reliance PBN30 are rated NEMA 3R like the steel PB30 and will not rust, which makes them better near salt air or salted roads. Steel resists impact better. Either is fine if it is listed, rated NEMA 3R and mounted where the cord cannot be tripped over.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far from the house should the generator be when plugged into the inlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers and safety agencies commonly cite at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents. That is why cord length matters: a 10-foot cord forces the generator too close in most layouts. Plan the generator spot first, then choose the inlet location and a 20 to 25-foot cord to match.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug a 50 amp generator into a 30 amp inlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not safely with an improvised adapter. The cord would be protected only by the generator's 50-amp breaker while carrying a 30-amp-rated connection. If the generator has an L14-30R as well as a 50-amp outlet, use the L14-30R with a 30-amp cord. If you want the full output, install a 50-amp inlet and matching transfer equipment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a power inlet box and a transfer switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The inlet box is the outdoor socket the generator cord plugs into. The transfer switch, or interlock, is the indoor equipment that disconnects the house from the grid and connects it to the generator instead. You need both. Some kits, like the Generac HomeLink 6853, include an inlet box with the switch, so check before buying one separately.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

