        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most homes with a portable generator, the Reliance Controls PB30 is the generator inlet box to buy: an L14-30 inlet that matches the twist-lock on most 5,000 to 9,000 watt generators, in a steel NEMA 3R box any electrician knows. Near salt air, take the Generac 6337 or Reliance PBN30 instead. For a generator with a true 50-amp outlet, the Reliance PBN50 is the pick.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The box is the cheap, easy part. The decisions that matter are matching the inlet to the generator's outlet, putting it where the generator can run safely, buying a cord long and heavy enough, and having it wired to a transfer switch or interlock under a permit. Get those right and the inlet will be the least interesting part of your next outage.
          </p>
